hf_eri_mask  @9b343f13
  why   Cross-checking runs two integral methods and compares them; a
        production run pays for one. The mask is a bit set of registry
        members, so the choice is made here and nowhere else.
  o     the run's options: `cross_check` and `eri_members` decide the mask
  m     the members the options ask for, as a whole number

hf_one_mask  @3b6b6308
  o     the run's options; only `cross_check` is read

hf_c2s  @f39a86e4
  why   The pipeline needs the same matrix for S, H and the repulsion
        integrals of a spherical run; this names it once.
  mol   the molecule; only its shells are read

hf_to_ao  @5223482a
  why   The integral methods work in Cartesian functions. A molecule asked
        to run in spherical functions (`5D`, `7F`) gets its one-electron
        matrices transformed here, through the same sweep that compares
        transform methods when cross-checking is on.
  X     a one-electron matrix over Cartesian functions, `[nc, nc]`
  mol   the molecule, which says whether the run is spherical
  o     the run's options, for the member mask
  rep   the sweep's report: how many methods ran and how far apart they were
  ns    the number of spherical functions

hf_overlap  @a6b7322a
  why   S is the metric of the basis: two basis functions on different
        atoms overlap, so the orbitals solve `F C = S C e` rather than an
        ordinary eigenproblem.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells and primitives
  o     the run's options: `cross_check` runs every member
  rep   the sweep's report
  n     the number of Cartesian basis functions
  S     the overlap matrix over Cartesian functions, `[n, n]`: `S[i, j]` is
        the integral of basis function i times basis function j

hf_hcore  @70c60354
  why   The core Hamiltonian is the part of the Fock matrix that does not
        depend on the density: the kinetic energy of one electron and its
        attraction to the nuclei.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells and primitives
  o     the run's options: `cross_check` runs every member
  rep   the sweep's report
  n     the number of Cartesian basis functions
  T     the kinetic energy matrix, `[n, n]`
  V     the nuclear attraction matrix, `[n, n]`: every nucleus, summed

hf_blob  @e9e65bfb
  why   The lock ladder and the suites build direct runs by hand; this is
        lib/hf/scf's blob under the name they use.
  mol   the molecule: atoms, shells, primitives, and whether it is spherical

hf_two_electron  @24513bcb
  why   The SCF loop takes one array for the two-electron part and hands it
        to the J/K builder the options pick; this puts the right thing in it.
  mol   the molecule
  o     the run's options; `jk` picks the J/K builder

hf_setup_of  @ce92c773
  why   The lock ladder computes the integrals itself and compares them with
        PySCF's before the SCF; this lets it build the setup from those
        (lib/hf/scf's `scf_setup_of`).
  mol   the molecule, for the electron counts and the nuclear repulsion
  H     the core Hamiltonian, `[n, n]`
  S     the overlap matrix, `[n, n]`
  G     the packed repulsion integrals, or the direct member's basis blob
  o     the run's options

hf_scf  @597a424c
  p     the setup: integrals, electron counts, nuclear repulsion and options

